"Monatshefte für Mathematik (https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10144793/1/Sokal2022_Article_TotalPositivityOfSomePolynomia.pdf)\nciteturn0search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Total positivity of some polynomial matrices ... Sokal1,2 ... polynomials · Rooted-forest polynomials · Schläﬂi–Gessel–Seo polynomials ·\nMonatshefte für Mathematik\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00605-022-01687-0\nTotal positivity of some polynomial matrices\nthat enumerate labeled trees and forests I:\nforests of rooted labeled trees\nAlan D. Sokal1,2\nReceived: 11 January 2022 / Accepted: 1 February 2022\n© The Author(s) 2022\nAbstract\nWe consider the lower-triangular matrix of generating polynomials that enumerate\nk-component forests of rooted trees on the vertex set [n] according to the number of\nimproper edges (generalizations of the Ramanujan polynomials). We show that this\nmatrix is coefﬁcientwise totally positive and that the sequence of its row-generating\npolynomials is coefﬁcientwise Hankel-totally positive. More generally, we deﬁne the\ngeneric rooted-forest polynomials by introducing also a weight m! φm for each vertex\nwith m proper children. We show that if the weight sequence φ is Toeplitz-totally\npositive, then the two foregoing total-positivity results continue to hold. Our proofs\nuse production matrices and exponential Riordan arrays.\nKeywords Tree · Labeled tree · Rooted tree · Forest · Rooted forest · Functional\ndigraph · Proper edge · Improper edge · Abel polynomials · Ramanujan\npolynomials · Rooted-forest polynomials · Schläﬂi–Gessel–Seo polynomials ·\nExponential generating function · Exponential Riordan array · Tree function,\nLambert W function · Production matrix · Toeplitz matrix · Hankel matrix · Totally\npositive matrix · Total positivity · Toeplitz-total positivity · Hankel-total positivity ·\nStieltjes moment sequence\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation 05A15 (Primary) · 05A19 · 05A20 · 05C05 ·\n05C30 · 15B05 · 15B36 · 15B48 · 30E05 · 44A60 (Secondary)\nCommunicated by Ilse Fischer.\nB Alan D. Sokal\nsokal@nyu.edu\n1\nDepartment of Mathematics, University College London, Gower Street, London WC1E 6BT, UK\n2\nDepartment of Physics, New York University, 726 Broadway, New York, NY 10003, USA\n123--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPositivity problems in enumerative and algebraic combinatorics (https://bishaldeb.com/positivity/themes/SMSCHTP/GKP-hankel.html)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; P1.10.1 GKP recurrence: Coefficientwise Hankel total positivity ... Origin: Posed by Alan Sokal (2014, unpublished) and [Salas--Sokal (2021), Conjecture 6.9].\n# Positivity problems in enumerative and algebraic combinatorics\n\n## Theme 1.10: Graham--Knuth--Patashnik recurrences and Stieltjes moment sequences, coefficientwise Hankel total positivity\n\n## Graham–Knuth–Patashnik (GKP) Recurrences\n\nGraham, Knuth, and Patashnik (GKP), in their book Concrete Mathematics (1994) posed the following \"research problem\" [Problem 6.94, pp. 319 and 564]:\nProblem: Develop a general theory of the solutions to the recurrence $$T(n,k)=(\\alpha n+\\beta k+\\gamma ){\\textstyle \\phantom{\\rule{0.167em}{0ex}}}T(n-1,k)+({\\alpha }^{\\prime }n+{\\beta }^{\\prime }k+{\\gamma }^{\\prime }){\\textstyle \\phantom{\\rule{0.167em}{0ex}}}T(n-1,k-1)$$ for $n\\ge 1$ and $k\\in \\mathbb{Z}$ with initial condition $T(n,k)={\\delta }_{k0}$.\n\nSee Barbero--Salas--Villaseñor (2014, 2015) and Salas--Sokal (2021).\n\nLet $A=\\{T(n,k){\\}}_{n,k\\ge 0}$ be a triangular array satisfying the GKP recurrence. Let ${P}_{n}(x)=\\sum _{k=0}^{n}T(n,k){x}^{k}$ denote its row-generating polynomial. Furthermore, we set $\\mathit{\\mu }=(\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma ,{\\alpha }^{\\prime },{\\beta }^{\\prime },{\\gamma }^{\\prime })$.\n\nKey References:\n\n  * J.F. Barbero G., J. Salas and E.J.S. Villaseñor, Bivariate generating functions fora class of linear recurrences: General structure J. Combin. Theory A125, 146--165 (2014).\n  * J.F. Barbero G., J. Salas and E.J.S. Villaseñor, Generalized Stirling permutations and forests: higher-order Eulerian and Ward numbers, Electron. J. Combin. 22, no. 3, paper 3.37 (2015).\n  * R. L. Graham, D. E. Knuth, O. Patashnik, Concrete Mathematics: A Foundation for Computer Science (2nd ed.). Addison-Wesley. (1994)\n  * J. Salas, A. D. Sokal, The Graham–Knuth–Patashnik Recurrence: Symmetries and Continued Fractions, Electron. J. Combin. 28(2), #P2.18 (2021).\n\nOpen\n\nP1.10.1 GKP recurrence: Coefficientwise Hankel total positivity\n\nConjecture: The sequence of polynomials ${({P}_{n}(x))}_{n\\ge 0}$ is coefficientwise Hankel totally positive in the seven variables $\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma ,{\\alpha }^{\\prime },{\\beta }^{\\prime },{\\gamma }^{\\prime },x$.\n\nOrigin: Posed by Alan Sokal (2014, unpublished) and [Salas--Sokal (2021), Conjecture 6.9].\n\nOpen\n\nP1.10.2 GKP recurrence: Coefficientwise Hankel total positivity\n\nIn place of the usual GKP recurrence, consider instead the following recurrence: $$\\stackrel{~}{T}(n,k)=(\\stackrel{~}{\\alpha }(n-1)+\\stackrel{~}{\\beta }k+\\stackrel{~}{\\gamma }){\\textstyle \\phantom{\\rule{0.167em}{0ex}}}\\stackrel{~}{T}(n-1,k)+({\\stackrel{~}{\\alpha }}^{\\prime }(n-1)+{\\stackrel{~}{\\beta }}^{\\prime }(k-1)+{\\stackrel{~}{\\gamma }}^{\\prime }){\\textstyle \\phantom{\\rule{0.167em}{0ex}}}\\stackrel{~}{T}(n-1,k-1)$$ and let ${\\stackrel{~}{P}}_{n}(x)=\\sum _{k=0}^{n}\\stackrel{~}{T}(n,k){x}^{k}$.\n\nConjecture: The sequence of polynomials ${({\\stackrel{~}{P}}_{n}(x))}_{n\\ge 0}$ is coefficientwise Hankel totally positive in the seven variables $\\stackrel{~}{\\alpha },\\stackrel{~}{\\beta },\\stackrel{~}{\\gamma },{\\stackrel{~}{\\alpha }}^{\\prime },\\stackrel{~}{{\\beta }^{\\prime }},\\stackrel{~}{{\\gamma }^{\\prime }},x$.\n\nOrigin: Posed by Alan Sokal (2014, unpublished) and [Salas--Sokal (2021), Conjecture 6.10].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTuran and Ramsey Problems for Alternating Multilinear Maps (https://opus.lib.uts.edu.au/rest/bitstreams/5ad73a54-0ce7-44d0-9854-c96f8194330d/retrieve)\nciteturn0search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Youming Qiao ... ** *There exists an alternating bilinear map* \\(\\phi : \\mathbb{F}^n \\times \\mathbb{F}^n \\to \\mathbb{F}^m\\), \\(n=\\Theta(s\\cdot t^2)\\), *such that* \\(\\phi\\) *has neither a dimension-\\(s\\) totally-isotropic space, nor a dimension-\\(t\\) complete space. ... On the other hand, by Equation 6, there exists an alternating bilinear map \\(\\phi : \\mathbb{F}^n \\times \\mathbb{F}^n \\to \\mathbb{F}^{2n}\\) with no totally-isotropic spaces of dimension \\(\\ge 2\\). ... Interestingly, Feldman and Propp termed their result as a “linear Ramsey theorem,” and compared it with the classical Ramsey theorem in [FP92, Sec.\nYouming Qiao\n\n**Proposition 5.2.** *There exists an alternating bilinear map* \\(\\phi : \\mathbb{F}^n \\times \\mathbb{F}^n \\to \\mathbb{F}^m\\), \\(n=\\Theta(s\\cdot t^2)\\), *such that* \\(\\phi\\) *has neither a dimension-\\(s\\) totally-isotropic space, nor a dimension-\\(t\\) complete space.*\n\n*Proof.* Let \\(m=\\left\\lfloor \\binom{t-1}{2} \\right\\rfloor\\). Let \\(n=\\left\\lfloor \\frac{(m+2)(s-2)}{2} \\right\\rfloor + 1\\), which implies that \\(\\frac{m+2n}{m+2} \\le s-1\\). Note that \\(n=\\Theta(s\\cdot m)=\\Theta(s\\cdot t^2)\\).\n\nBy Ol’shanskii ([Ol’78, Lemma 2]) and Buhler, Gupta, and Harris ([BGH87, Main Theorem]), \\(\\alpha_{\\mathbb{F}}(n,m,2)\\le \\left\\lfloor \\frac{m+2n}{m+2} \\right\\rfloor\\). It follows that there exists \\(\\phi : (\\mathbb{F}^n)^{\\times \\ell}\\to \\mathbb{F}^m\\) with \\(\\alpha(\\phi)\\le s-1\\). Furthermore, by \\(m=\\left\\lfloor \\binom{t-1}{2} \\right\\rfloor\\), any complete spaces of \\(\\phi\\) is of dimension \\(\\le t-1\\). The result then follows. □\n\nClosing the gap between \\(\\Omega(s\\cdot t^2)\\) from Proposition 5.2, and \\(s\\cdot t^4\\) from Theorem 1.1, is an interesting open problem.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTotal positivity of some polynomial matrices that enumerate labeled trees and forests I: forests of rooted labeled trees (https://d-nb.info/125968038X/34)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Crawled: 4.6 years ago; Sokal¹,² ... **Keywords** Tree · Labeled tree · Rooted tree · Forest · Rooted forest · Functional digraph · Proper edge · Improper edge · Abel polynomials · Ramanujan polynomials · Rooted-forest polynomials · Schläfli–Gessel–Seo polynomials · Exponential generating function · Exponential Riordan array · Tree function, Lambert W function · Production matrix · Toeplitz matrix · Hankel matrix · Totally positive matrix · Total positivity · Toeplitz-total positivity · Hankel-total positivity · Stieltjes moment sequence\nMonatshefte für Mathematik  \nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00605-022-01687-0\n\nTotal positivity of some polynomial matrices that enumerate labeled trees and forests I: forests of rooted labeled trees\n\nAlan D. Sokal¹,²\n\nReceived: 11 January 2022 / Accepted: 1 February 2022  \n© The Author(s) 2022\n\n**Abstract**  \nWe consider the lower-triangular matrix of generating polynomials that enumerate *k*-component forests of rooted trees on the vertex set [n] according to the number of improper edges (generalizations of the Ramanujan polynomials). We show that this matrix is coefficientwise totally positive and that the sequence of its row-generating polynomials is coefficientwise Hankel-totally positive. More generally, we define the generic rooted-forest polynomials by introducing also a weight *m!* φₘ for each vertex with *m* proper children. We show that if the weight sequence φ is Toeplitz-totally positive, then the two foregoing total-positivity results continue to hold. Our proofs use production matrices and exponential Riordan arrays.\n\n**Keywords** Tree · Labeled tree · Rooted tree · Forest · Rooted forest · Functional digraph · Proper edge · Improper edge · Abel polynomials · Ramanujan polynomials · Rooted-forest polynomials · Schläfli–Gessel–Seo polynomials · Exponential generating function · Exponential Riordan array · Tree function, Lambert W function · Production matrix · Toeplitz matrix · Hankel matrix · Totally positive matrix · Total positivity · Toeplitz-total positivity · Hankel-total positivity · Stieltjes moment sequence\n\n**Mathematics Subject Classification** 05A15 (Primary) · 05A19 · 05A20 · 05C05 · 05C30 · 15B05 · 15B36 · 15B48 · 30E05 · 44A60 (Secondary)\n\nCommunicated by Ilse Fischer.\n\nAlan D. Sokal  \nsokal@nyu.edu\n\n1 Department of Mathematics, University College London, Gower Street, London WC1E 6BT, UK  \n2 Department of Physics, New York University, 726 Broadway, New York, NY 10003, USA\n\nPublished online: 21 March 2022  \nSpringer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-concavity and log-convexity in the theory of the Graham--Knuth--Patashnik recurrences (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/408521155_Log-concavity_and_log-convexity_in_the_theory_of_the_Graham--Knuth--Patashnik_recurrences)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Conjecture 1.6 (Sokal [18]). ... mials of the GKP recurrence (1.1) is coeﬃcientwise Hankel-totally positive, jointly in\n\ncoeﬃcientwise partial order in x.\n\nHere we consider the row-generating polynomials P n(x;µ) arising from the GKP\n\nrecurrence (1.1) with parameters µ= (α, β, γ , α′, β′, γ′), which are regarded as inde-\n\nterminates. The partially ordered commutative ring is (Z[x, µ],⪰), where ⪰is the\n\ncoeﬃcientwise partial order in the seven variables x, α, β, γ, α′, β′, γ′. In this context,\n\nSokal [18] has conjectured that\n\nConjecture 1.6 (Sokal [18]).The sequence (P n(x;µ))n≥0 of row-generating polyno-\n\nmials of the GKP recurrence (1.1) is coeﬃcientwise Hankel-totally positive, jointly in\n\nall seven indeterminates x and α, β , γ, α′, β′, γ′.\n\nThis conjecture is still open. However, for some particularizations of the param-\n\neters µ, the coeﬃcientwise Hankel-totally positivity has been proven [16]: e.g., for\n\nµ= (α, 0, γ, α′,0, γ′), µ= (α, β, γ, 0, β′,−β′), or µ= (0,β,γ,0, β′, γ ′).\n\nWe note that Eq. (1.15) implies that the sequence (P n(x;µ))n≥0 is coeﬃcientwise\n\nHankel-totally positive of order 1. On the other hand, a generic 2 ×2 minor of the\n\nHankel matrix (1.18) is given by\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nP i+j P i+j+q\n\nP i+j+p P i+j+p+q\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n=P i+j P i+j+p+q−P i+j+p P i+j+q,(1.19)\n\nwhere i, j ≥0 and p, q ≥1. Then, the positivity of any 2 ×2 minor of the Hankel\n\nmatrix is equivalent to the condition\n\nP n(x;µ)P n+p+q(x;µ)−P n+q(x;µ)P n+p(x;µ)⪰0 (1.20)\n\nfor all n, p, q such that n≥0 and p, q ≥1. But this expression is equivalent to\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTur\\'an and Ramsey problems for alternating multilinear maps (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343253177_Tur%27an_and_Ramsey_problems_for_alternating_multilinear_maps)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; For alternating bilinear maps over F p,p a prime, complete spaces and totally- ... of complete spaces and anisotropic spaces play the role of cliques, two Ramsey ... Let φ:F n×F n→F n be the alternating matrix space given to us.\n\nZippel lemma [Sch80,Zip79]. In [BCG+20], it is shown that the problem of de-\n\nciding whether an alternating bilinear map is anisotropic subsumes the problem\n\nof deciding quadratic residuosity modulo squarefree composite numbers, a diﬃcult\n\nnumber-theoretic problem.\n\n4.3. Ramsey numbers for alternating multilinear maps. Based on which\n\nof complete spaces and anisotropic spaces play the role of cliques, two Ramsey\n\nnumbers can be deﬁned for alternating multilinear maps.\n\nDeﬁnition 4.1. For a ﬁeld F and s, t, ℓ ∈N,s, t ≥ℓ≥1, the Ramsey number for\n\ncomplete spaces, R c\n\nF(s, t, ℓ), is the mininum number n∈N such that any alternating\n\nℓ-linear map φ: (F n)×ℓ→F m has either a totally-isotropic space of dimension s,\n\nor a complete space of dimension t.\n\nThe Ramsey number for anisotropic spaces, R a\n\nF(s, t, ℓ), is deﬁned in the same\n\nway, except that we replace complete spaces with anisotropic spaces in the above.\n\n8 YOUMING QIAO\n\nIt is clear that R a\n\nF(s, t, ℓ)≤R c\n\nF(s, t, ℓ).\n\nAs with hypergraph Ramsey numbers, the ﬁrst question is whether R c\n\nF(s, t, ℓ) and\n\nR a\n\nF(s, t, ℓ) are ﬁnitely upper bounded, and if so, provide an explicit bound as tight\n\nas possible. Improving bounds for hypergraph Ramsey numbers is a ma jor research\n\ntopic in Ramsey theory, with classical works by Ramsey [Ram30] and Erd˝os and\n\nRado [ER52], and the recent breakthrough by Conlon, Fox, and Sudakov [CFS10].\n\nHowever, directly applying those methods for the hypergraph Ramsey theorem\n\nseems not working. One reason is that the “correlation” in the linear algebraic\n\nworld prohibits the divide and conquer paradigm. For example, one way to prove\n\nthe ℓ-uniform hypergraph Ramsey theorem is to realise the recursive relation for\n\nRamsey numbers as\n\nR(s, t, ℓ)≤R(R(s−1, t, ℓ),R(s, t −1, ℓ), ℓ −1) + 1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTotal positivity of some polynomial matrices that enumerate labeled trees and forests I: forests of rooted labeled trees (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00605-022-01687-0)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Crawled: last week; This known fact is a specialization of the claim in Conjecture 6.1(b) that the sequence of polynomials P_n(x;a,b)$P_n(x;a,b)$ is coefficientwise Hankel-totally positive. ... So Conjecture 6.1(b) does not have an analogue involving division by n!.\n### Conjecture 6.1\n\n(Total positivities for the Schläfli–Gessel–Seo polynomials).\n\n  1. (a)\n\nThe unit-lower-triangular polynomial matrix P(a,b) = \\bigl ( P_{n,k}(a,b) \\bigr )_{n,k \\ge 0}$P(a,b) = \\bigl ( P_{n,k}(a,b) \\bigr )_{n,k \\ge 0}$ is coefficientwise totally positive (jointly in a, b).\n\n  2. (b)\n\nThe polynomial sequence {\\varvec{P}}= \\bigl ( P_{n}(x;a,b) \\bigr )_{n \\ge 0}${\\varvec{P}}= \\bigl ( P_{n}(x;a,b) \\bigr )_{n \\ge 0}$ is coefficientwise Hankel-totally positive (jointly in x, a, b).\n\n  3. (c)\n\nThe polynomial sequence {\\varvec{P}}^\\triangle = \\bigl ( P_{n+1,1}(a,b) \\bigr )_{n \\ge 0}${\\varvec{P}}^\\triangle = \\bigl ( P_{n+1,1}(a,b) \\bigr )_{n \\ge 0}$ is coefficientwise Hankel-totally positive (jointly in a, b).\n\nI have verified part (a) up to 15 \\times 15$15 \\times 15$, and part (b) up to 11 \\times 11$11 \\times 11$; part (c) is an immediate consequence of part (b). Conjecture 6.1 of course implies analogous statements for the Rothe polynomials (6.7b), but not conversely.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCoefficientwise Hankel-total positivity from a universal cumulant lifting (https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01172)\nciteturn0academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; We establish composition, binomial-convolution, and substitution results, and apply them to implicit equations and autonomous differential equations, tree and forest families, and lognormal compound-Poisson moments in a unified approach. ... We not only give new proofs of coefficientwise Hankel-total positivity of many combinatorial polynomials, but also prove coefficientwise Hankel total positivity for the component polynomials of functional digraphs confirming a conjecture of Sokal and establish fixed-shape Schläfli--Gessel--Seo polynomials positivity supporting another conjecture of Sokal.\nTitle: Coefficientwise Hankel-total positivity from a universal cumulant lifting\nAuthors: Bao-Xuan Zhu\nDate: Sat Oct 31 18:18:57 2026\n\nUsing probabilistic methods, we establish universal cumulant lifting criteria for Stieltjes and Hamburger moment sequences. In the Stieltjes case, we characterize coefficientwise Hankel total positivity after convolution with every initial moment sequence by requiring each once-shifted cumulant sequence to be Stieltjes. We also prove equivalence with the numerical moment property at every nonnegative intensity. In the Hamburger case, we use twice-shifted cumulants to obtain coefficientwise-positive leading Hankel determinants, a degree bound, an explicit highest-degree component, and drift invariance. We work with formal series and require neither exponential moments nor moment determinacy. We establish composition, binomial-convolution, and substitution results, and apply them to implicit equations and autonomous differential equations, tree and forest families, and lognormal compound-Poisson moments in a unified approach. From known moment sequences, we obtain general coefficientwise Hankel-totally positive families containing the classical Bessel, Lah, and Laguerre polynomials. We not only give new proofs of coefficientwise Hankel-total positivity of many combinatorial polynomials, but also prove coefficientwise Hankel total positivity for the component polynomials of functional digraphs confirming a conjecture of Sokal and establish fixed-shape Schläfli--Gessel--Seo polynomials positivity supporting another conjecture of Sokal.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite boundedness of Ramsey numbers for complete and anisotropic spaces — MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/347351/finite-boundedness-of-ramsey-numbers-for-complete-and-anisot)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Finite boundedness of Ramsey numbers for complete and anisotropic spaces ... For an alternating $\\ell$ℓ-linear map $\\phi:(\\mathbb{F}^n)^{\\times\\ell}\\to\\mathbb{F}^m$ϕ:(F n)×ℓ→F m, define $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{c}}_{\\mathbb{F}}(s,t,\\ell)$R F c​(s,t,ℓ) as the least $n$n such that every such map has either a totally isotropic space of dimension $s$s or a complete space of dimension $t$t. ... source: Youming Qiao, “Turán and Ramsey problems for alternating multilinear maps”, arXiv:2007.12820 (2023).\n\nFinite boundedness of Ramsey numbers for complete and anisotropic spaces\n\nConjecture openOpen about 6 years · documented by 2020\n\nLet $\\mathbb{F}$F be a field and let $s,t,\\ell\\in\\mathbb{N}$s,t,ℓ∈N satisfy $s,t\\geq\\ell\\geq2$s,t≥ℓ≥2. For an alternating $\\ell$ℓ-linear map $\\phi:(\\mathbb{F}^n)^{\\times\\ell}\\to\\mathbb{F}^m$ϕ:(F n)×ℓ→F m, define $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{c}}_{\\mathbb{F}}(s,t,\\ell)$R F c​(s,t,ℓ) as the least $n$n such that every such map has either a totally isotropic space of dimension $s$s or a complete space of dimension $t$t. Define $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{a}}_{\\mathbb{F}}(s,t,\\ell)$R F a​(s,t,ℓ) similarly, replacing complete spaces by anisotropic spaces.\n\nRamsey-number conjecture. The Ramsey numbers for complete spaces and anisotropic spaces, $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{c}}_{\\mathbb{F}}(s,t,\\ell)$R F c​(s,t,ℓ) and $\\mathrm{R}^{\\mathrm{a}}_{\\mathbb{F}}(s,t,\\ell)$R F a​(s,t,ℓ), are upper bounded by explicit functions in $s$s, $t$t, and $\\ell$ℓ.\n\nAt present, finite upper bounds are not known for general $\\ell$ℓ. The conjecture asks for explicit bounds analogous to those in hypergraph Ramsey theory, despite the additional linear-algebraic correlations that obstruct the usual recursive arguments.\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\nsource: Youming Qiao, “Turán and Ramsey problems for alternating multilinear maps”, arXiv:2007.12820 (2023).\n\nalternating-multilinear-mapsfinite-fieldsramsey-theory\n\n0\n\nposted by arXiv 3 days ago\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenConjecture/openco